@@ -163,6 +163,7 @@ before evaluating a candidate built from that cache.
 | 0954 | `server`: on PS5, an opened file's directory is resolved with `realpath` once and kept (64 entries, checked by device and inode), so an open costs two system calls instead of one a path component. From PS5_MihawkOS (its 2080) |
 | 0955 | `ntdll`: on PS5, the directories a process resolved are kept by their upper-cased Windows path (1024 slots) and a lookup starts from the longest one, checked with one `fstatat`; a directory's case sensitivity is not asked (the console's disk is). From PS5_MihawkOS (its 2081) |
 | 0956 | `gdi32`: with a native `opengl32` (Mesa), setting a window's pixel format again to the one it has succeeds instead of failing: Godot 4 sets it twice and stopped with "Failed to create an OpenGL window". From Playden's prospero-win |
+| 0957 | `server`: on PS5 every file is the player's, the console's one user: a file owned by another id than `getuid()`'s, as one the game had just made could be, was Anonymous's, and its security descriptor gave the user no access. Java's `Files.isWritable`, which asks `AccessCheck`, took a new save for read-only, and Necesse's zip file system refused to write the world |
 | 0958 | `ntdll`: `WINE_CPU_TOPOLOGY`'s processor count (as Proton reads it) is reported as `NumberOfProcessors` when it is between 1 and the CPUs the process has (0881); logged. From Playden's prospero-win |
 | 0959-0963 | `vkd3d-shader`: fx_2_0 effects compile with pass assignments, sampler state initializers, `true`/`false` for any state, null shader and texture states, and ps_1_x shaders as ps_2_0, so D3DX9 effects from source build. From Playden's prospero-win |
 | 0971 | `kernelbase`: in a title, an unhandled exception doesn't start a debugger (which can't start there) and ends the process after the game's own filter: reading AeDebug took the process heap's lock a faulting thread could hold, and the crash froze the game instead of ending it. From Playden's prospero-win |
@@ -187,6 +188,10 @@ before evaluating a candidate built from that cache.
 | 0999 | `win32u`: on PS5, the user driver hides the pointer once the player hasn't moved it or clicked for `WINE_PS5_CURSOR_IDLE_MS` milliseconds (unset or 0 never; PC5 sets 5000 for a game whose Mouse pointer option says so), both where it draws the cursor over the screen and where Vulkan frames get it from VideoOut's hardware cursor (0740); the next move, even against an edge, or click shows it again, as does the game moving it to another place itself (`SetCursorPos`; Grim Dawn moves its pointer by the controller). A game setting the same place or a new image doesn't count, and the cursor the application set is unchanged for `GetCursorInfo`. A game played with the controller that leaves the Windows cursor on showed it, still, in a corner (Valor Mortis). |
 | 0964 | `kernelbase`: for a console program started without a console window (`CONSOLE_HANDLE_SHELL_NO_WINDOW`, as the title starts every game), a screen buffer query on one of its standard handles that fails answers an empty 80x25 buffer, and filling it or moving its cursor succeeds without doing anything. .NET's `Console.Clear()` threw "The handle is invalid" there and Streets of Rage 4 ended at start; a real console and other handles are answered as before |
 | 0965 | `win32u`: setting a pixel format on the desktop window no longer posts `WM_WINE_SETPIXELFORMAT` to it. Nothing owns the server-created desktop on the PS5, so the message came back to the game's own queue and posted itself again forever. `vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR` sets format -1 on every surface's window, so a Vulkan surface on the desktop (DXVK or Zink) kept the game on a black screen with one core busy (Syndicate) |
+| 0966 | `win32u` (prospero-win 0998, Leo Lobato): the adapter's 32-bit `HardwareInformation.MemorySize` is capped at 0xffffffff. On the Unix side of a 64-bit build `ULONG_MAX` is 64 bits wide, so the cap never applied and a GPU with an exact multiple of 4 GiB, as the PS5's 16 GiB, wrote 0 there, and WMI's `AdapterRAM` was 0: launchers took it for a machine below their minimum (Batman: Arkham City) |
+| 0967 | `ntdll` (Mihawk OS 2427): `NtQueryInformationProcess(ProcessCommandLineInformation)`, class 60, which Windows answers from 8.1 on, gives the command line: a `UNICODE_STRING` whose buffer follows it, from the process's parameters, its own or another process's through its PEB; `wow64` converts it as it does the image name. It was an unknown class, and a launcher that checks the command line of the game it started read nothing |
+| 0968 | `ntdll`: `GetModuleHandle` of an API set (`api-ms-win-crt-heap-l1-1-0.dll`) whose host in system32 isn't loaded answers with a loaded module of the host's name. A game that loads its own `ucrtbase.dll` from its folder got NULL, which Windows, with one module of that name, never answers, and Company of Heroes 3 then wrote to address 0 on purpose |
+| 0969 | `ntdll`: `GetThreadTimes` on the current thread reads the thread's own CPU clock where the platform has no per-thread times (the PS5), not the whole process's. On PS5 (Mihawk OS 2457), `GetCurrentProcessorNumber` is RDTSCP's processor id, the CPU the thread runs on, as on Windows, kept below the number of processors reported: the console has no `sched_getcpu`, and the fallback gave every thread processor 0, so a program keeping data per processor put every thread on one slot |
 | 0974 | `ntdll`: outside images, a page made read-only or inaccessible takes writing, or all access, away from its whole 16 KiB host page, so touching it faults as on Windows; the host page used to get the union of its pages' protections, and a write to a protected page beside a writable one raised nothing (DuckStation then ran stale recompiled code). An access that faults only because of such a neighbour is single-stepped with the host page open for that one instruction, unseen by the program. On a PC, `WINE_HOST_PAGE_SIZE=16384` keeps 16 KiB host pages to run this scheme (`tests/test_wine_subpage_protect.py`) |
 | 0975 (upstream 0986) | `ntdll`: accesses that 0974 steps over beside a protected page are counted, reads too. When a host page has had 256 of them, a write beside a read-only page is still reported there, and any other access gives the host page the union of its pages' protections, as before 0974, until a protection in it changes. Beside an inaccessible page nothing ended the stepping: Unity's Mono garbage collector (Darkwood) took about 13,000 steps a second and ran at 5 fps |
 | 0976 | `ntdll` (Mihawk OS 2085): on PS5, a boolean socket option (`SO_BROADCAST`, `SO_KEEPALIVE`, `SO_REUSEADDR` and the other boolean `SOL_SOCKET` ones, `TCP_NODELAY`) reads back as 0 or 1, as on Windows and Linux. The console's FreeBSD kernel answered with the option's flag bit (`SO_BROADCAST` as 0x20), so a program that compares the value with TRUE took a set option for unset |
diff --git a/native/wine64_main.c b/native/wine64_main.c
index 72be166..25ab5c5 100644
--- a/native/wine64_main.c
+++ b/native/wine64_main.c
@@ -968,6 +968,18 @@ static unsigned long long cpu_ms(void)
            (unsigned long long)(usage.ru_utime.tv_usec + usage.ru_stime.tv_usec) / 1000u;
 }
 
+/* The process's file system blocks read and written so far: a stall while a
+ * game saves shows whether the disk is busy (Postal 2's two seconds). */
+static void io_blocks(long *in, long *out)
+{
+    struct rusage usage;
+
+    *in = *out = 0;
+    if (getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF, &usage)) return;
+    *in = usage.ru_inblock;
+    *out = usage.ru_oublock;
+}
+
 /* ---- launcher ----------------------------------------------------------- */
 
 /* Shown until a game is chosen; Wine is not loaded. Does not return. */
@@ -2296,17 +2308,19 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
         }
         if (tick % 60 == 0) {
             uint64_t v[16] = { 0 };
+            long blocks_in, blocks_out;
 
+            io_blocks(&blocks_in, &blocks_out);
             PS5LOG_LOG("PW_WINE64 alive tick=%llu sink_calls=%lu frames_put=%llu shown=%llu "
                        "rejected=%llu last=%ux%u inputs=%llu refused=%llu vk_shown=%llu vk_busy=%llu vk_kept=%llu "
-                       "vk_held=%llu cpu_ms=%llu audio=%lu audible=%lu peak=%d",
+                       "vk_held=%llu cpu_ms=%llu audio=%lu audible=%lu peak=%d blocks_in=%ld blocks_out=%ld",
                        (unsigned long long)tick, sink_calls, (unsigned long long)frames.sequence,
                        (unsigned long long)shown, (unsigned long long)frames.rejected,
                        frames.width, frames.height, (unsigned long long)posted,
                        (unsigned long long)refused, (unsigned long long)vulkan_frames.shown,
                        (unsigned long long)vulkan_frames.busy, (unsigned long long)vulkan_frames.kept,
                        (unsigned long long)vulkan_frames.held, cpu_ms(),
-                       audio_grains, audio_audible, audio_peak);
+                       audio_grains, audio_audible, audio_peak, blocks_in, blocks_out);
             audio_peak = 0;
             if (start.virtual_stats && tick % 300 == 0) {
                 static uint64_t faults_logged;
diff --git a/tools/build_mesa_zink.md b/tools/build_mesa_zink.md
index 688599e..307d6c3 100644
--- a/tools/build_mesa_zink.md
+++ b/tools/build_mesa_zink.md
@@ -32,6 +32,12 @@ bash tools/build_mesa_zink.sh --work build/mesa-zink \
   --jobs 1
 ```
 
+`--patches DIR` applies the `*.patch` files of DIR, in name order, to a copy
+of the exported source (`source-patched` in the work directory), which is built
+instead; the export itself stays as it is and is still validated. A different
+set of patches makes the copy and both builds again. PC5 keeps its Zink patches
+in `patches/zink` and passes them here.
+
 Both architectures are built sequentially. Keep sufficient memory for the
 release link; the first dual build was validated with one job and an 8 GiB
 memory limit. Reuse the same work directory only for this pinned recipe.
diff --git a/tools/build_mesa_zink.sh b/tools/build_mesa_zink.sh
index fd031f0..841d6a1 100755
--- a/tools/build_mesa_zink.sh
+++ b/tools/build_mesa_zink.sh
@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@ root=$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)
 MESA_COMMIT=e4607ef697fe3df8d1ebc329bf1917d041843306
 LLVM_MINGW_VERSION=20260922-ucrt
 LLVM_MINGW_SHA256=bb7bb7654b33d5aa8712acb837c963b2e0c56352560c76105270a3268c665c21
-work= mesa= archive= jobs=1
+work= mesa= archive= patches= jobs=1
 fail() { echo "build_mesa_zink: $*" >&2; exit 2; }
 while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case $1 in
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@ while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  --mesa) mesa=$2; shift 2;;
  --llvm-mingw) archive=$2; shift 2;;
  --jobs) jobs=$2; shift 2;;
+ --patches) patches=$2; shift 2;;
  *) fail "unknown argument $1";;
  esac
 done
@@ -33,6 +34,31 @@ fi
 [ "$(cat "$work/source/.revision")" = "$MESA_COMMIT" ] || fail 'work source pin differs; use a new work directory'
 python3 "$root/tools/mesa_zink_manifest.py" --verify-source "$work/source" "$mesa" "$MESA_COMMIT"
 [ "$(cat "$work/source/VERSION")" = 26.2.0 ] || fail 'unexpected Mesa version'
+# --patches DIR: its *.patch, in name order, on a copy of the source; the pinned
+# export stays as it is, verified. A different set makes the copy and the builds again.
+src=$work/source
+if [ -n "$patches" ]; then
+ patches=$(realpath "$patches")
+ set -- "$patches"/*.patch
+ [ -f "$1" ] || fail "no patches in $patches"
+ id=$(cat "$@" | sha256sum | cut -c1-64)
+ src=$work/source-patched
+ if [ "$(cat "$src/.patches" 2>/dev/null)" != "$id" ]; then
+  rm -rf "$src" "$work/build-i686" "$work/build-x86_64"
+  staging=$(mktemp -d "$work/source-patched.XXXXXX")
+  cp -a "$work/source/." "$staging/"
+  for patch in "$@"; do
+   GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES=$work git -C "$staging" apply --whitespace=nowarn "$patch" || fail "patch does not apply: ${patch##*/}"
+  done
+  printf '%s\n' "$id" > "$staging/.patches"
+  mv "$staging" "$src"
+ fi
+fi
+# Builds stay with the source they were set up for.
+if [ "$(cat "$work/.source-used" 2>/dev/null)" != "$src" ]; then
+ rm -rf "$work/build-i686" "$work/build-x86_64"
+ printf '%s\n' "$src" > "$work/.source-used"
+fi
 if [ ! -f "$work/toolchain/.archive-sha256" ]; then
  staging=$(mktemp -d "$work/toolchain.XXXXXX")
  tar -xJf "$archive" -C "$staging" --strip-components=1
@@ -64,7 +90,7 @@ needs_exe_wrapper = true
 CROSS
  build=$work/build-$arch
  reconfigure=(); [ ! -f "$build/build.ninja" ] || reconfigure=(--reconfigure)
